gateAs: initialise ASL so "ALLOW <asg>" without a level works

readPvList() declares `int lev;` uninitialised and only assigns it when an
ASL token is present, or when no ASG is given at all.  A pvlist line of the
form

    tcs:.* ALLOW DEFAULT

(ASG present, ASL absent - the form the pvlist documentation describes as
defaulting to level 1) takes neither branch, so the entry is created with an
indeterminate level: carried over from the previous line, or read from
uninitialised stack on the first such line.

gateAsEntry::level is passed to asAddClient() as the client's access security
level, and asLibRoutines.c skips any rule where
`pasgclient->level > pasgrule->level`.  A level above 1 therefore causes every
RULE(1,READ)/RULE(1,WRITE) to be skipped, and the gateway reports "no read, no
write" to every client matching that pattern while still serving the PV
normally - connected, correct native type and element count.  Which ASG the
pvlist names makes no difference, so the fault is immune to access-file
changes, survives restarts, and varies between process invocations.

Observed in production: a gateway proxying a telescope control system denied
all clients for four weeks after a host reboot, with no configuration change.
Diagnosis was slow because both config files were correct and ascheck(1)
reported no errors.

Fix: initialise lev at declaration; reset it per line alongside the existing
pattern/rname/hname reset; fall back to the documented default of 1 when no
ASL token is present; and reject out-of-range values with a message instead of
silently denying all access.

Note the per-line reset is the substantive omission - the loop resets the
three pointers each iteration but not the int, which is how a value leaks from
one pvlist line to the next.

Signed-off-by: Hawi Stecher <hawi.stecher@noirlab.edu>
